            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"NOVEL\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The firmament is understood as the created expanse or vault of the sky that separates the cosmic waters above from those below. Symbolically, it represents God's ordering of chaos, the manifestation of His glory, and the spatial or spiritual boundary between the earthly realm and God's transcendent heavenly throne. Alternatives include various literal cosmological views and a few patristic allegories (such as representing Scripture or the Church).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The firmament is the place where the celestial lights are hung, functioning symbolically as a divider between two 'seas of people': the restless earthly nations below and the gathered heavenly assembly above.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The physical sky or celestial vault separating cosmic waters, symbolizing the boundary between earthly and divine realms.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The sky serving as a symbolic divider between two specific populations: earthly nations below and the heavenly assembly above.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"NO_CONSENSUS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"NO_BASELINE\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus views the firmament as separating literal cosmic waters or dividing the spatial/spiritual earthly and heavenly realms. The book introduces a specific typological reading that redefines the waters as populations, making the firmament a divider between earthly nations and the heavenly assembly.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus does not contain the interpretation that the waters above and below represent two seas of people (earthly nations and a heavenly assembly).\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book acknowledges the physical aspect of the firmament ('where the lights are hung') but assigns it a primary symbolic meaning based on cross-referencing Genesis 1 with Revelation 17:15. Because no consensus response or listed alternative interprets the firmament as a divider of human and angelic populations, the reading is novel.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Revelation 17:15 explicitly interprets the 'waters' in a specific apocalyptic vision (where the harlot sits) as peoples and nations. Applying this specific apocalyptic decoding backward to redefine the cosmic waters of Genesis 1 requires a significant interpretive leap that the texts themselves do not demand.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
